>
    <h1 style="margin-top: 2rem; font-size: 3rem; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
        Mechanics
    </h1>

    <h2>Deck Mechanics</h2>

    <h3>Opal-Wave Permanent Exile</h3>
    <p>
        With Opalescence and Parallax Wave on the board, Wave is a 4/4 that can
        target itself. You hold priority and stack a bunch of creature-exile
        triggers, then use the last counter to exile Wave itself. Wave comes
        back with five fresh counters as a new object. Then all the
        creature-exile triggers resolve and those creatures are gone
        permanently. Repeat until the board is clear.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Combat Sequencing:</strong> A side effect of this trick is that the
        Wave returns as a new object, meaning it has summoning sickness and cannot
        attack that turn. Therefore, whenever possible, you should temporarily exile
        blockers normally, swing with your team (including the Wave) for 4 damage,
        and <em>then</em> do the permanent exile trick in your second main phase.
        Those 4 points of damage can often change the outcome of the game. The exception
        is if the opponent's blockers have powerful Enter the Battlefield (ETB) effects
        (like <em>Goblin Ringleader</em> or <em>Bone Shredder</em>), because
        temporarily exiling them means they'll return and trigger their ETB
        again before being permanently exiled. Against those, you just
        permanent-exile them immediately.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Exiling Opposing Enchantments:</strong> Because Opalescence affects <em>all</em> non-Aura enchantments on the battlefield, your opponent's enchantments also become creatures. This means you can use the Wave trick to permanently exile problematic enchantments like <em>Survival of the Fittest</em>, <em>Sylvan Library</em>, or an opposing <em>Seal of Cleansing</em>.
    </p>

    <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Opalescence & Summoning Sickness</h3>
    <p>
        It's a common mistake for newer players to think they can cast Replenish and immediately swing for lethal. Because Opalescence turns your enchantments into creatures, any enchantment that enters the battlefield this turn <em>will have summoning sickness</em>. You cannot attack the turn you cast Replenish. This is why the deck relies on Parallax Wave and Decree of Silence—you need to lock the opponent out to survive a full turn cycle before you can attack.
    </p>

    <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">The Decree Lock</h3>
    <p>
        Post-Replenish with all three pieces, creatures are permanently exiled
        and spells are countered.
    </p>

    <h3>Resetting Decree with Wave</h3>
    <p>
        When the third depletion trigger goes on the stack (which would
        sacrifice Decree), you respond with Wave to exile the Decree. It comes
        back with three fresh counters. Your opponent gets a small window, but
        they'd need to have had three spells countered and still have an instant
        ready. Very few decks can pull that off. Worst case they destroy your
        Opalescence through the window, and Decree comes back as a regular
        enchantment to counter their next three spells.
    </p>

    <h3>Value Replenish</h3>
    <p>
        You don't always need the full combo. I've won plenty of games by just
        getting back a single Decree mid-game to choke an opponent who has a
        hand full of expensive spells. Opal plus Wave by itself stabilizes
        against most creature boards.
    </p>

    <h3>Protecting Your Board</h3>
    <p>
        If someone casts Tranquil Domain or Wrath of God, you can respond with
        Wave (while Opal is on board) to exile your own enchantments. They come
        back fresh when Wave exits. If you have two Opalescences in play and
        they target one with removal, you can Wave the targeted one. It comes
        back as a new object and the removal fizzles.
    </p>

    <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Parallax Wave + Disenchant Effect</h3>
    <p>
        Just like the Opalescence trick, you can permanently exile creatures
        using Seal of Cleansing or a sideboard Disenchant. Activate Parallax
        Wave to target up to 5 of your opponent's creatures. While those
        activations are still on the stack, sacrifice your Seal of Cleansing
        targeting your own Wave. The Wave leaves the battlefield, its "return
        creatures" trigger resolves (returning nothing), and then the exile
        triggers resolve, permanently removing the creatures. This is a crucial
        survival tactic in the early game.
    </p>

    <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Parallax Wave Creature Synergies</h3>
    <p>
        Parallax Wave isn't just for clearing your opponent's board; it has
        incredible synergy with your own creatures, especially post-sideboard. <em
            >Note: The first two tricks practically require you to have both
            Opalescence and Parallax Wave on the board so you can instantly
            flicker the Wave and get your creatures back immediately.</em
        >
    </p>
    <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
        <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
            <strong>Flipping Exalted Angel for Free:</strong> If you cast Exalted
            Angel face-down as a morph (for 3 mana), you can target it with Parallax
            Wave. Then, use the Opalescence trick to flicker the Wave. When the Angel
            returns to the battlefield, it comes back face-up. This instantly saves
            you the 2WW unmorph cost.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
            <strong>Resetting Meddling Mage:</strong> If your opponent draws an out
            to the card you named with Meddling Mage, or if you simply need to lock
            out a different spell, you can exile your own Mage with Wave and immediately
            flicker the Wave. When the Mage returns, you get to name a brand-new card.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
            <strong>Creature Protection:</strong> This trick only requires the Wave.
            You can use Parallax Wave as a "safe house" for your Mages or Angels in
            response to spot removal or a sweeper. Exile them yourself, and let the
            Wave fade naturally later to bring them back completely unharmed.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Asymmetrical Tsabo's Web</h3>
    <p>
        The deck leverages Tsabo's Web to shut down problematic lands like
        Rishadan Port, Wasteland, and Dust Bowl. The reason this card is
        completely asymmetrical is that it only affects lands with activated
        abilities that <em>don't</em> produce mana. All of our non-basic lands (Skycloud
        Expanse, Adarkar Wastes, Ancient Tomb) only have mana abilities, meaning they
        untap perfectly fine under the Web.
    </p>

    <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Frantic Search Mana Tricks</h3>
    <p>
        Frantic Search isn't just a discard outlet; the "untap up to three
        lands" clause enables several powerful mana tricks:
    </p>
    <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
        <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
            <strong>The 'Free' Discard:</strong> Because the spell untaps your lands,
            it acts as a completely free way to dump combo pieces into your graveyard
            on the turn you intend to "go off." Even better, if one of the three lands
            you untap is an Ancient Tomb, it actually nets you +1 mana. For example:
            tap three lands (including Tomb) to float 4 mana, spend 3 on Frantic Search,
            and untap those three lands. You now have 1 floating + 4 from lands =
            5 mana total on turn 3, giving you enough to cast Replenish and still
            hold up interaction.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
            <strong>Beating Rishadan Port:</strong> If an opponent tries to tap your
            land with Port during your upkeep, you can float the mana in response,
            let the port trigger resolve and then cast Frantic Search using that mana.
            The spell will untap the lands, effectively ignoring the Port activation.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
            <strong>Beating Tangle Wire:</strong> Similar to the Port trick, if you
            have 3 mana sources, you can float all 3 mana in response to the Tangle
            Wire tap trigger on your upkeep. Let the trigger resolve, tapping your
            permanents. Then, use the 3 floating mana to cast Frantic Search, untapping
            three of those lands.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Attunement's Built-in Protection</h3>
    <p>
        The ability of Attunement reads: "Return Attunement to its owner's hand:
        Draw three cards, then discard four cards." Because returning it to your
        hand is the <em>cost</em> to activate the ability (and not the effect), you
        can always activate it in response to a Disenchant or Seal of Cleansing. The
        Attunement immediately goes to your hand, dodging the removal completely,
        and you still get your card filtering.
    </p>

